## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with non-uniform skip constraints and reconstruction, common in scheduling and resource-constrained selection. It ensures you can encode constraints as a forward DP and extract the chosen indices.

## Key Concepts

- DP with forward state (i, r)
- Transition skipping vs taking with jump i+gap[i]
- Path reconstruction and complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumWithGaps(A, gap, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const memo = Array.from({ length: n + 2 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  const go = (i, r) => {
    if (r === 0) return 0;
    if (i > n) return NEG;
    if (memo[i][r] != null) return memo[i][r];
    const skip = go(i + 1, r);
    const take = A[i - 1] + go(Math.min(n + 1, i + gap[i - 1]), r - 1);
    const best = Math.max(skip, take);
    memo[i][r] = best;
    return best;
  };

  const best = go(1, k);
  return { best, memo };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for variable k_max instead of exact k? 
- How would you reconstruct the indices efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends the classic Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS) problem by allowing up to k character deletions, requiring a 3D DP formulation. It tests understanding of subsequence DP with additional constraints, careful boundary handling, and the ability to reconstruct the solution path.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Palindromic Subsequence with deletions
- 3D DP (i, j, d) where d represents remaining deletions
- Path reconstruction for deletion decisions
- Time/space complexity: O(n²·k) time, O(n²·k) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function longestPalindromicSubseqWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0))
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
      dp[i][i][d] = 1;
    }
  }

  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], (len > 2 ? dp[i + 1][j - 1][d] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (d > 0) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], dp[i + 1][j][d - 1], dp[i][j - 1][d - 1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[0][n - 1][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: parity constraint on subarrays adds a practical twist to classic DP without changing core technique.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two dimensions (i, t)
- Ending a subarray with even length using prefix sums
- Path reconstruction and memory optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEvenSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let l = 2; l <= i; l += 2) {
          const val = dp[i-l][t-1] + (S[i] - S[i-l]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            choice[i][t] = l;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

